{{!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SECTION 1: Hero
     Top gradient banner (gradient-svg-generator) → portrait + H1 + taglines
     + proof line → personal sea photo with caption → mini visitor counter.
     2026-08-12: the hero mark is a <picture>, not a bare <img>. The monkey is
     drawn in #070607 on transparency, so on GitHub's dark theme the first thing
     a visitor saw was a black shape on a near-black page. Light readers keep the
     transparent original — no card, no border, exactly as before — and dark
     readers get chan-monkey-live-on-white.svg, the same animation on a white
     card. The prefers-color-scheme hook does not exist inside an <img>-loaded
     SVG, but it does exist here in the host markup, which is why this surface
     can have both instead of picking one.
     Verified through GitHub's own Markdown API, not by assumption: it keeps
     <picture>/<source>/srcset AND the align="right" on the inner <img>, so the
     float and the text wrap survive. It wraps the whole thing in
     <themed-picture>, which is inline and does not disturb the float.
     Both SVGs are VENDORED from the chan-meng-logo repo (assets/anime/), which
     owns the artwork and the derivation — do not edit or regenerate them here.
     Upstream: `npm run build:on-white`, then copy the result over.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}

{{> banner decorations.banners.hero}}

<a href="{{basics.url}}"><picture><source media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" srcset="/public/brands/chan-monkey-live-on-white.svg"><img src="/public/brands/chan-monkey-live.svg" align="right" width="160" alt="{{basics.name}} — {{basics.label}} · {{default basics.location.region "Auckland, New Zealand"}}"></picture></a>

*{{#each (split basics.taglines.heroLabel "|")}}{{trim this}}{{#unless @last}} · {{/unless}}{{/each}}.*
*{{default basics.location.region "Auckland, New Zealand"}}.*

{{_valueProposition.tagline}} {{basics.taglines.primary}}

